{0 Install}

This page sets up a project and makes a first component. The
{{!page-tutorial}tutorial} then adds a Remote call, a client file and a server.

{1:requirements Requirements}

- OCaml 5.1 or later and dune 3.20 or later.
- Melange 7 or later, for the code that runs in the browser.
- mlx, the JSX syntax for OCaml: the opam packages [mlx] (the commands
  [mlx-pp] and [ocamlmerlin-mlx]) and [ocamlformat-mlx].
- [jsonkit] and [jsonkit-melange], when the app derives its codecs (see
  {!page-recipes.json}).
- [tiny_httpd], for the server adapter [silica.adapter].
- Node.js, for esbuild and Tailwind in the client build.

Silica is not on opam yet. Pin it with
[opam pin add silica git+https://github.com/davesnx/silica#main] (and the same
for [silica-ui]), or put a checkout inside your dune workspace. The pin also
installs the command [silica].

{1:new A new app}

[silica new myapp] makes a complete app in the directory [myapp]: a counter
component with a Remote call in [app/shared/], a server in [server/], a client
in [client/], two tests in [test/], the checks in [check/] and an
[AGENTS.md] with the commands to build, run, test and check it. From a checkout of the repository, run
[dune exec -- silica new ../myapp].

{@sh skip[
cd myapp
npm --prefix client install
silica dev
]}

[silica dev] builds the app, runs the server, and reloads the page after each
save (see {{!section-dev}Development}).

[dune test] runs the tests: one steps the machine, one drives the page with
{!Silica_memory}, and [check/main.ml] runs the checker and compares the golden
files in [check/spec/]. [silica check] runs the checker alone, and
[silica check --json] prints its report as JSON (see
{{!page-checker.suite}Check a project}).

[silica new] never overwrites a file. A file that already has the content of
the template is skipped. When a file differs, the command lists it and writes
nothing.

{1:dev Development: silica dev}

[silica dev] runs an app while you edit it. One command builds the app on
each save, starts the server again, swaps the new code into the page and keeps
the state of its machines (hot reload), and shows compile errors in the page.
An agent with no browser reads the same events as JSON lines.

{2:dev-run Run}

From the root of an app that [silica new] made:

{@sh skip[
npm --prefix client install
silica dev
]}

Then open {{:http://127.0.0.1:8080}http://127.0.0.1:8080}. The options, with
their defaults, match the layout of [silica new]:

- [--server server/main.exe]: the server executable, as a dune target.
- [--client client]: the client directory. dune builds [client/meta.json] and
  [client/dist] (esbuild).
- [--port 8080]: the port of the page, on 127.0.0.1.
- [--json]: print each event as one JSON line on stdout.

In this repository, run the site with
[dune exec -- silica dev --server site/server/main.exe --client site/client].

{2:dev-what What it does}

- It starts [dune build --watch] on the server and the client and reads the
  build state and the errors from dune's RPC socket ([_build/.rpc/dune]).
- After each build that succeeds, it compares the server executable and
  [meta.json] with the last ones. When one changed, it stops the server with
  SIGTERM (SIGKILL after 3 s), starts the new one on a free port, and waits
  for [GET /_silica/health] to answer 200 (at most 10 s).
- It listens on [--port] and passes each connection to the server. A request
  that arrives while the server starts waits for it, at most 10 s. When no
  server runs, a page shows the build state and reloads when the server is
  up.
- It tells the page what changed: a new script is swapped in with no page
  load (see Hot reload below); a new stylesheet alone replaces
  the [<link>] in place; a change to the server alone reloads nothing, and the
  Live sockets connect again by themselves.

The server gets this environment: [PORT], [SILICA_SECRET] (made once and kept
in [_build/silica-dev/secret]), [SILICA_ORIGINS], [SILICA_STATIC],
[SILICA_METAFILE] and [SILICA_DEV_STATE] ([_build/silica-dev/state.json]).
With [SILICA_DEV_STATE], {!Silica_server.dev_of_env} gives [Some _]: the
server saves its sessions, its Live islands and its kept values at SIGTERM and
reads them at start (see {!Silica_server.Make.shutdown}), so the cookie, the
CSRF token and the state of each island stay across a restart. A hot reload
keeps the instance id of each island, so an island keeps its state; a full
page reload makes new island instances. In dev mode each HTML page also loads
[/_silica/dev/client.js], the dev client.

The server of [silica new] reads all of these. A server of your own must read
[PORT], [SILICA_STATIC], [SILICA_METAFILE], use
{!Silica_server.secret_of_env} and {!Silica_server.dev_of_env}, and call
[Server.shutdown] in the [on_stop] of {!Silica_adapter.run}. An app with Live
islands also calls the codec registration of its machines at start, so the
islands can be saved.

{2:dev-page In the page}

The dev client connects to [ws://<host>/_silica/dev]. It shows:

- a small indicator while dune builds, with the number of rules done;
- an overlay when the build fails: each error with its file, line and column,
  the message, and two source lines on each side. An error with no location
  (an esbuild or a Tailwind failure) shows its message only. The page under
  the overlay keeps running the last good build. The overlay goes at the next
  build that succeeds;
- an overlay with the output of the server when the server stops.

The dev client is a separate script, served by [silica dev]; it is never in
the app bundle. A test in [test/budget] checks the bundles for it.

{2:dev-hot Hot reload}

After a build that changed the client, the page stops the running code (its
timers, calls, Live sockets, held sources and listeners), keeps the snapshot of
each machine instance by its instance path, loads the new bundle, and resumes
every instance whose state type did not change: the new view shows, the DOM
nodes that did not change stay (so focus and scroll stay), and the timers and
calls of each snapshot start again (a timer with its full delay, a call is
sent again). An instance starts again, and a panel in the page names it with
the reason, when its state type changed, when it has no fingerprint, when its
state holds a function, or when the new view raises on the old snapshot. The
other instances keep their state. When the new code does not start within 5
s, the page reloads.

A machine keeps its state only when it passes a fingerprint of its state type
to [Machine.make]: [[@@deriving fingerprint]] on the state type gives
[state_fingerprint], and the machine passes [~fingerprint:state_fingerprint]; a
type from another module that the state mentions needs
[[@@deriving fingerprint]] in its own module (see {!Silica.Fingerprint} and
{!page-machines.make}). The hot reload code is in the
development bundle only: the dev esbuild rule of the client injects
[out/node_modules/silica.browser/hot_reload.mjs], and a test checks that the
release bundles have none of it.

{2:dev-json Events as JSON}

With [--json], stdout has one JSON object per line. The server output goes to
stderr. The events:

- [{"t":"dev","url":"http://127.0.0.1:8080"}]: [silica dev] listens.
- [{"t":"build","status":"started"}], then [{"t":"build","status":"ok"}] or
  [{"t":"build","status":"failed","errors":[...]}]. Each error has [file]
  (relative to the root), [line], [col] (from 1), [end_line], [end_col],
  [severity] (["error"] or ["warning"]), [message] and [excerpt], a list of
  [[line, text]]. An error with no location has [severity] and [message]
  only. When dune reports more errors after the first [failed], another
  [failed] follows with all of them.
- [{"t":"server","status":"started","pid":4021,"ms":20}], or ["restarted"].
- [{"t":"server","status":"crashed","code":"exit 2","output":"..."}]: the
  server stopped. [output] is its last 60 lines. [silica dev] starts it again
  after the next build that succeeds, not before.
- [{"t":"reload","what":"code","entry":"/static/client_main-HASH.js","css":[...]}]:
  the page swaps in the new bundle; ["page"] reloads the page; ["css"] swaps
  the stylesheets only.
- [{"t":"hot-reload","status":"kept","ms":26,"kept":[...],"restarted":[...]}]:
  the page resumed the new code in [ms]. [kept] lists the instance paths that
  kept their state; each [restarted] entry has [path], [machine] and [reason],
  such as ["its state type changed"]. [{"t":"hot-reload","status":"reloaded","reason":...}]:
  the page fell back to a full reload.

An edit, a compile error and its fix of the example app:

{v
{"t":"build","status":"started"}
{"t":"build","status":"ok"}
{"t":"server","status":"restarted","pid":784319,"ms":20}
{"t":"reload","what":"code","entry":"/static/client_main-PN2V6BRI.js","css":[]}
{"t":"hot-reload","status":"kept","ms":26,"kept":["router","router/:about"],"restarted":[]}
{"t":"build","status":"started"}
{"t":"build","status":"failed","errors":[{"file":"example/app/shared/example_about.mlx","line":20,"col":20,"end_line":20,"end_col":32,"severity":"error","message":"Unbound constructor About_broken","excerpt":[[18,"    (Node.element \"h1\""],[19,"       [ Attr.bool \"data-silica-route-focus\" true ]"],[20,"       [ Node.text About_broken ]"],[21,"    )"],[22,"    (Example_nav.make ())"]]}]}
{"t":"build","status":"started"}
{"t":"build","status":"ok"}
v}

The fix gave the same files as the build before the error, so the server did
not start again. An agent waits for [ok] and, after a change to the server or
the client, for [restarted], and after a change to the client for
[hot-reload] with the instances kept and restarted.

{2:dev-stop Stop}

SIGINT (Ctrl-C), SIGTERM and SIGHUP stop the server (which saves its state),
then dune, and free the port; the exit code is 0. The children run in their
own process groups, so SIGKILL on [silica dev] leaves them running.

A second [silica dev] in the same project stops at once with
[another silica dev runs in this project (pid N)]. A [dune build --watch]
that already runs in the project stops it too: [silica dev] starts its own.

The rest of this page sets up a project by hand.

{1:project The dune project}

The project file declares the mlx dialect, so that dune reads [.mlx] files as
OCaml with JSX:

From [dune-project]:
{@dune skip[
(dialect
 (name mlx)
 (implementation
  (extension mlx)
  (merlin_reader mlx)
  (format
   (run ocamlformat-mlx %{input-file}))
  (preprocess
   (run mlx-pp %{input-file}))))
]}

It also needs [(using melange 1.0)] at the top.

Code that the server and the browser share goes in one folder with no stanza.
Two libraries copy it: one for native OCaml and one for Melange. The examples
of this documentation use this setup. The native library:

From [docs/examples/native/dune]:
{@dune skip[
(copy_files ../shared/*.{ml,mlx})

(library
 (name silica_docs_examples_native)
 (wrapped false)
 (modes byte native)
 (libraries silica silica-ui)
 (preprocess
  (pps silica.ppx)))
]}

The Melange library is the same with [(modes melange)] and the preprocessors
[silica.ppx melange.ppx]. The ppx [silica.ppx] turns the JSX elements into
calls of {!Silica.Node.element} (see {!page-components.jsx}), and gives the
deriver [[@@deriving fingerprint]] (see {!page-machines.make}). An app that
derives JSON codecs needs a different library for each side; see
{!page-recipes.json}.

The browser entry is a [melange.emit] stanza over the Melange library and
[silica.browser]:

{@dune file=examples/client/dune[
(melange.emit
 (target out)
 (alias docs-examples-js)
 (modules tutorial_client)
 (module_systems
  (esm mjs))
 (libraries silica_docs_examples_melange silica.browser silica.json-melange))
]}

A bundler such as esbuild makes the file for the page from the output of this
stanza. See [example/client/dune] in the repository for the esbuild rule, and
{!page-recipes.hosting} for the server.

{1:first A first component}

A component is a machine and a view. This lamp has two states, [Off] and
[On], and one event, [Toggle]. The states are a plain variant, and the
function [name] gives the name of each state. The view shows a text and a
button for each state.

{@mlx file=examples/shared/doc_lamp.mlx[
open Silica

type event = Toggle
type state = Off | On

let name = function Off -> "off" | On -> "on"

let machine =
  Machine.make ~id:"lamp" ~init:Off ~name (fun state Toggle ->
      match state with Off -> Go On | On -> Go Off
  )

let view : state -> event Node.t = function
  | Off ->
      <div>
        <p>"The lamp is off."</p> <button on_press=Toggle>"Turn on"</button>
      </div>
  | On ->
      <div>
        <p>"The lamp is on."</p> <button on_press=Toggle>"Turn off"</button>
      </div>

let component = Component.make machine view
]}

The same code runs on native OCaml. A test can step the machine and print the
HTML of the view:

{@ocaml[
# let lamp = Doc_lamp.machine;;
val lamp : (unit, Doc_lamp.state, Doc_lamp.event) Silica.Machine.t = <abstr>
# let state, _ = Silica.Machine.start lamp ();;
val state : Doc_lamp.state = Doc_lamp.Off
# let state, _, _ = Silica.Machine.step lamp () state Doc_lamp.Toggle;;
val state : Doc_lamp.state = Doc_lamp.On
# Silica.Render.to_string (Doc_lamp.view state);;
- : string = "<div><p>The lamp is on.</p><button>Turn off</button></div>"
]}

In the browser, one call of {!Silica_browser.mount} paints it into an element
of the page. The {{!page-tutorial.browser}tutorial} shows the client file.

